# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Small-scale simulator example {#small-scale-simulator-example}"
msgstr "Small-scale simulator example {#small-scale-simulator-example}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Like other learning-based error mitigation methods, PEC with shaded lightcones mitigates the *physical* noise of a specific quantum processor, so it depends on hardware services with no meaningful analog on an ideal simulator:\n"
msgstr "Tulad ng iba pang learning-based error mitigation methods, ang PEC na may shaded lightcones ay nagpapagaan sa *physical* na noise ng isang specific quantum processor, kaya umaasa ito sa hardware services na walang makabuluhang analog sa isang ideal simulator:\n"

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "`NoiseLearnerV3` experimentally characterizes the sparse Pauli-Lindblad noise channel on each unique two-qubit layer. On a noiseless simulator there is no noise to cancel.\n"
msgstr "Ang `NoiseLearnerV3` ay experimental na nagkaka-characterize ng sparse Pauli-Lindblad noise channel sa bawat unique two-qubit layer. Sa isang noiseless simulator, walang noise na kakailanganing kanselahin.\n"

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, no-wrap
msgid "The `Executor` primitive samples the twirled, anti-noise-injected circuits generated by `samplomatic` on a backend.\n"
msgstr "Ang `Executor` primitive ay nagsa-sample ng twirled, anti-noise-injected circuits na ginenerate ng `samplomatic` sa isang backend.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The shaded-lightcone bound computation is classical, but it is only meaningful relative to the learned hardware noise rates, which set the mitigation budget and sampling overhead. For these reasons we skip the small-scale simulator example and demonstrate the full PEC+SLC workflow directly on hardware, with each step of the Qiskit pattern broken out below.\n"
msgstr "Ang shaded-lightcone bound computation ay classical, ngunit makabuluhan lamang ito kaugnay ng learned hardware noise rates, na siyang nagtatakda ng mitigation budget at sampling overhead. Dahil dito, nilalaktawan natin ang small-scale simulator example at direktang ipinapakita ang buong PEC+SLC workflow sa hardware, na may bawat step ng Qiskit pattern na binreak down sa ibaba.\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Large-scale hardware example {#large-scale-hardware-example}"
msgstr "Large-scale hardware example {#large-scale-hardware-example}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We run the complete PEC+SLC workflow on a 20-qubit mirrored Ising circuit executed on IBM Quantum&reg; hardware, following the four steps of a Qiskit pattern.\n"
msgstr "Pinapatakbo natin ang kumpletong PEC+SLC workflow sa isang 20-qubit mirrored Ising circuit na na-execute sa IBM Quantum&reg; hardware, sinusunod ang apat na step ng isang Qiskit pattern.\n"

# doq: kept in English by the translator (name or code)
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Step 1: Map the problem {#step-1-map-the-problem}"
msgstr "Step 1: Map the problem {#step-1-map-the-problem}"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Our demonstration is set up as follows:\n"
msgstr "Ganito ang setup ng ating demonstration:\n"

#. type: Bullet: '1. '
#, no-wrap
msgid "We choose to perform time evolution under a 1D Ising chain Hamiltonian. The 1D Ising chain gives a nicely dense circuit structure, which is convenient for showcasing PEC implementations.\n"
msgstr "Pinipili nating magsagawa ng time evolution sa ilalim ng isang 1D Ising chain Hamiltonian. Ang 1D Ising chain ay nagbibigay ng magandang siksik na circuit structure, na convenient para sa pagpapakita ng mga PEC implementation.\n"

#. type: Bullet: '2. '
#, no-wrap
msgid "We choose to measure the observable $\\langle X_6 Z_{13}\\rangle$, which contains one $X$ and one $Z$ component. This is convenient for highlighting how the SLC bounds treat different types of errors differently.\n"
msgstr "Pinipili nating sukatin ang observable na $\\langle X_6 Z_{13}\\rangle$, na naglalaman ng isang $X$ at isang $Z$ component. Convenient ito para maipakita kung paano magkaiba ang pagtrato ng SLC bounds sa iba't ibang uri ng errors.\n"

#. type: Bullet: '3. '
#, no-wrap
msgid "We want the ideal expectation value of this observable to be exactly 1, so that the quality of the mitigated estimates is easy to judge. Mirroring the circuit gets us most of the way there: for every gate in the second half of the circuit there is an inverse gate in the first half, so that the ideal circuit acts as the identity.\n"
msgstr "Gusto natin na ang ideal expectation value ng observable na ito ay eksaktong 1, para madaling husgahan ang kalidad ng mitigated estimates. Ang pag-mirror sa circuit ay ang karamihan ng solusyon dito: para sa bawat gate sa second half ng circuit, may inverse gate sa first half, kaya ang ideal circuit ay kumikilos bilang identity.\n"

#. type: Bullet: '4. '
#, no-wrap
msgid "Finally, obtaining the ideal expectation value of 1 requires the circuit to start &mdash; and therefore, thanks to the mirroring, ideally also end &mdash; in a +1 eigenstate of the observable. Because the observable contains a non-$Z$ component, and the `Executor` primitive accounts for the desired measurement basis at the end of the circuit, we prepend the corresponding basis-preparation gates at the start of the mirror circuit with a `prepare_basis` function. We use the `get_measurement_bases` function to identify which gates are needed and where to place them, while keeping track of qubit-index subtleties arising from `box` annotation conventions, as discussed in the section on preparing canonical basis measurements.\n"
msgstr "Sa huli, ang pagkakaroon ng ideal expectation value na 1 ay nangangailangan na ang circuit ay magsimula &mdash; at dahil dito, dahil sa mirroring, ideal din na magtapos &mdash; sa isang +1 eigenstate ng observable. Dahil ang observable ay naglalaman ng non-$Z$ component, at ang `Executor` primitive ay isinasaalang-alang ang gustong measurement basis sa dulo ng circuit, idinagdag natin ang kaukulang basis-preparation gates sa simula ng mirror circuit gamit ang `prepare_basis` function. Ginagamit natin ang `get_measurement_bases` function para matukoy kung anong mga gates ang kailangan at kung saan ilalagay ang mga ito, habang sinusubaybayan ang mga subtlety sa qubit-index na nagmumula sa `box` annotation conventions, tulad ng tinalakay sa seksyon tungkol sa paghahanda ng canonical basis measurements.\n"

